        "n": 3,
        "title": "Reading Passage 3: The persistence and peril of misinformation",
        "topic": "communication",
        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "id": "P1",
            "text": "Brian Southwell looks at how human brains verify information and discusses some of the challenges of battling widespread falsehoods."
          },
          {
            "id": "P2",
            "text": "Misinformation - both deliberately promoted and accidentally shared - is perhaps an inevitable part of the world in which we live, but it is not a new problem. People likely have lied to one another for roughly as long as verbal communication has existed. Deceiving others can offer an opportunity to gain strategic advantage, to motivate others to action, or even to protect interpersonal bonds. Moreover, people inadvertently have been sharing inaccurate information with one another for thousands of years."
          },
          {
            "id": "P3",
            "text": "However, we currently live in an era in which technology enables information to reach large audiences across the globe, and thus the potential for immediate and widespread effects from misinformation now looms larger than in the past. Yet the means to correct misinformation might, over time, be found in those same patterns of mass communication and of the facilitated spread of information."
          },
          {
            "id": "P4",
            "text": "The main worry regarding misinformation is its potential to unduly influence attitudes and behaviour, leading people to think and act differently than they would if they were correctly informed, as suggested by the research teams of Stephan Lewandowsky of the University of Bristol and Elizabeth Marsh of Duke University. We worry that misinformation might lead people to hold false beliefs and that these, especially when occurring among large groups of people, may have harmful consequences for health, social harmony, and the political climate."
          },
          {
            "id": "P5",
            "text": "At least three observations related to misinformation in today's mass-media environment deserve attention. First, people who encounter misinformation tend to believe it, at least initially. Secondly, electronic and print media often do not block many types of misinformation before it reaches large audiences. Thirdly, countering misinformation once it has been widely shared can be a resource-intensive effort."
          },
          {
            "id": "P6",
            "text": "Knowing what happens when people initially encounter misinformation holds tremendous importance. Although it is fairly routine for individuals to come across information that is false, the question of exactly how - and when - we mentally label information as true or false has long been debated. The dilemma is neatly summarised by a contrast between how the 17th-century philosophers René Descartes and Baruch Spinoza described human information engagement. Descartes argued that a person only accepts or rejects information after considering its truth; Spinoza argued that people accept all information (or misinformation) by default and then subsequently verify or reject it. In recent decades, empirical evidence from Erik Asp of the University of Chicago and Daniel Gilbert at Harvard University has supported Spinoza's account: people appear to encode all new information as if it were true, even if only momentarily, and later tag the information as either true or false. This is consistent with the observation that mental resources for scepticism physically reside in a different part of the brain than the resources used in perceiving and encoding."
          },
          {
            "id": "P7",
            "text": "What about the fact that misinformation often appears in the media without being blocked? In the United States, regulatory agencies tend to focus on post hoc detection. Organisations such as the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) monitor and notify, but rarely censor content beforehand. The FDA oversees prescription drug advertising and runs the \"Bad Ad\" programme, which allows people to report violations of drug-risk guidelines. Such programmes are useful but do not prevent false advertising from airing. Moreover, even misinformation that is later corrected can continue to shape attitudes."
          },
          {
            "id": "P8",
            "text": "This leads us to the third observation: correcting misinformation requires significant resources. To be persuasive, corrective campaigns must be clear and accessible, requiring effort to frame messages well or to educate audiences about misinformation. Media literacy efforts may even need to start in elementary school. Yet people still struggle to distinguish \"fake news\" from factual reporting."
          },
          {
            "id": "P9",
            "text": "We live in a time when misinformation is common. Yet many people are also passionately working on solutions. Future remedies will require not only theory but consistent monitoring tools and a recognition that claims lacking scientific consensus or social reality should be countered. Misinformation arises from human fallibility and information needs. Overcoming its worst effects will demand coordinated, long-term effort rather than a one-time cure."
          }
        ],
